nigeria's president mohammad isaf voted  to choose his successor  after two five-year terms he could have  run for the third term  but is choosing to step down while other  leaders in the region  desperately cling to power the election  is coming at a difficult time for his  nation  a point not lost on the outgoing  president  nigeria is facing immense challenges  security challenges  demographic challenges climate  challenges economic and social  development challenges  including the immediate sanitary  challenge posed by kobit-19  i'm convinced that to face these  challenges we need strong and stable  democratic institutions  as voting is underway seven electoral  officials were killed in tillaberry  a region closed tunisia borders with  mali  and burkina faso their vehicle was hit  by a roadside bomb  that also injured three others there had  been fears of violence even before the  boat  especially in the region that seen  several attacks by armed groups  this is the man the ruling party chose  to take over from the incumbent  muhammad bazum a trusted ally of  president yusufu  and former minister of both the interior  and foreign ministries  i have just voted after spending several  weeks in the provinces  i visited several villages and had a lot  of contact and i wish for calm during  this election  discipline and that the winner be  blessed with good fortune  i wish for good fortune to be on my side  of course and have many reasons to  believe that it is on my side  i therefore look ahead to the future  with serenity and a lot of calm  his challenger is nigeria's former  president and prime minister  maman usman was running for the fifth  time seeking to come back to the post  he held 25 years ago guzman who voted in  his home region of zinder  warned against election fraud  if citizens notice that these elections  have once more been fraudulent elections  i fear that the situation will be  difficult to manage last time people  were disciplined  we ourselves contributed to making sure  it was calm and serene and tolerated the  results of a fraudulent election  but this time since you asked me my  feelings my feeling is that the  population is no longer ready to  tolerate fraudulent elections  in the first round of parliamentary and  presidential elections held on december  27th  mohammed bazum secured 39.3 of the vote  while osman got 16.9 percent but without  a clear majority  it forced a runoff ahead of sunday's  election the two candidates went into  strategic alliances with nij's smaller  political parties  with a view to forming coalitions  analysts suggest the governing party  whose party won 80 of the 171 seats in  parliament  may age out the opposition which has  only seven seats  but the candidate of the opposition  insist the voters  that have the final say the result is  expected in a matter of days  when the election commission declares a  winner two weeks after that  the constitutional court will validate  or reject the results as announced  but for many voters what's important now  is to have a leader  who can quickly address the problems of  poverty unemployment  and violence by armed groups ahmedi ruiz  al jazeera
 

